%I A398715 #17 Aug 13 2026 22:11:51
%S A398715 5186,5816,9185,9815,51586,51686,51986,58516,58616,58916,91585,91685,
%T A398715 91985,98515,98615,98915,511686,511886,512686,512886,515586,518186,
%U A398715 518286,519186,519286,581616,581816,582616,582816,585516,588116,588216,589116,589216,911685
%N A398715 Integers that need two iterations of 180-degree Rotate and Add to reach a strobogrammatic number (7-segment calculator-style numerals).
%C A398715 Let R(k) denote the integer obtained by rotating the decimal representation of k by 180 degrees using 7-segment calculator-style numerals, with digit map 0 -> 0, 1 -> 1, 2 -> 2, 5 -> 5, 6 -> 9, 8 -> 8, 9 -> 6, and reversing the order of the digits. R(k) is defined here only when every digit of k is in {0,1,2,5,6,8,9} and k does not end in 0, so that the rotated representation has no leading zero.
%C A398715 Let T(k) = k + R(k), whenever R(k) is defined. The sequence consists of the positive integers k such that k is not strobogrammatic, T(k) is not strobogrammatic, R(T(k)) is defined, and T(T(k)) is strobogrammatic in the sense of A018846.
%C A398715 The sequence is infinite. For every m >= 0, the decimal number consisting of 511 followed by m zeros followed by 686 is a term.
%C A398715 If k is a term, then R(k) is also a term, because k and R(k) have the same first iterate: k + R(k).
%C A398715 This is a rotational analog of A065207.
%H A398715 <a href="/index/Res#RAA">Index entries for sequences related to Reverse and Add</a>.
%e A398715 5186 is a term because R(5186) = 9815 and 5186 + 9815 = 15001, which is not strobogrammatic.
%e A398715 Then R(15001) = 10051 and 15001 + 10051 = 25052, which is strobogrammatic. Therefore exactly two iterations are needed.
%e A398715 5816 is also a term because R(5816) = 9185 and 5816 + 9185 = 15001, giving the same second iteration.
%e A398715 56 is not a term of this sequence because 56 + R(56) = 56 + 95 = 151 is already strobogrammatic after one iteration.
%e A398715 511686 illustrates the infinite family: 511686 + 989115 = 1500801, 1500801 + 1080051 = 2580852, and 2580852 is strobogrammatic.
%o A398715 (Python)
%o A398715 rot = {
%o A398715     "0": "0",
%o A398715     "1": "1",
%o A398715     "2": "2",
%o A398715     "5": "5",
%o A398715     "6": "9",
%o A398715     "8": "8",
%o A398715     "9": "6"
%o A398715 }
%o A398715 def R(n):
%o A398715     s = str(n)
%o A398715     if s[-1] == "0" or any(c not in rot for c in s):
%o A398715         return None
%o A398715     return int("".join(rot[c] for c in reversed(s)))
%o A398715 def is_strobogrammatic(n):
%o A398715     r = R(n)
%o A398715     return r is not None and r == n
%o A398715 def is_term(n):
%o A398715     r = R(n)
%o A398715     if r is None or is_strobogrammatic(n):
%o A398715         return False
%o A398715     x = n + r
%o A398715     if is_strobogrammatic(x):
%o A398715         return False
%o A398715     rx = R(x)
%o A398715     return rx is not None and is_strobogrammatic(x + rx)
%o A398715 def terms(limit):
%o A398715     return [n for n in range(1, limit + 1) if is_term(n)]
%o A398715 print(terms(1000000))
%Y A398715 Cf. A018846 (strobogrammatic numbers), A398714 (need one iteration).
%Y A398715 Cf. A065207.
%K A398715 nonn,base,new
%O A398715 1,1
